(* Go+ v0.13.0 grammar delta: propositions as hypotheses.

   The complete base grammar is Go plus the declarations and expressions in
   grammar-v0.12.0.ebnf. No new syntax: this file records a change in what
   an existing form MEANS.
*)

(*
  v0.151.0 made a bound statable but left it inert — a proposition in
  scope did nothing. Two changes make it work.

  - **A proposition in scope is a hypothesis.** The enclosing function's
    own quantity-0 proposition parameters are supplied to the decider when
    it discharges a call's obligation, so propositions compose: under
    `Lt[1, n]`, both `Le[1, n]` and `Lt[1, n+1]` follow with no new proof.
    The decider always accepted hypotheses (`Decide(goal, hyps)`); the
    proof path simply passed none.

  - **An erased index may be forwarded to a call.** A quantity-0 parameter
    could not be named in any runtime position, and a call argument is
    indistinguishable from one in pass 1, so an index could be written
    only as a literal — which made composition unreachable. A quantity-0
    name is now admitted as a call argument. Passing one to a genuine
    runtime parameter is still refused: it is absent from the generated
    signature, so the strict go/types backstop rejects the reference, and
    that error now explains the erasure rather than only reporting an
    undefined name.

  A proposition is still not a hypothesis for MATCH refinement: `Lt[i, n]`
  does not tell an exhaustiveness check that `n` is non-zero. That, and
  user-declared predicates, remain.

  KNOWN UNSOUNDNESS (predates this delta; see GOALS.md Stage B): omitting
  a proof argument entirely skips its obligation, because a call whose
  erased arguments were never written is textually identical to one whose
  arguments have already been erased by a previous pass. `Cast(v)` is
  accepted with no proof and no assumption recorded. Closing it needs the
  obligation settled while the call site is still pristine.
*)
